{"schema_version": 4, "name": "killing-assay-cytotoxicity", "title": "In-vitro-style killing / cytotoxicity assay", "created": "2026-09-08", "status": "complete", "design_status": "complete", "implementation_status": "complete", "simulation_status": "ran", "phase": "Evaluate", "question": "In a well-mixed assay, do active T cells produce net tumor cell death (positive\ncytotoxicity) relative to a matched no-T control, robustly across seeds?\n", "study_card": {"goal": "Reproduce the paper's in-vitro killing readout: positive cytotoxicity vs. a matched control.", "mechanism": "Well-mixed tumor cells (50% PDL1+, MHCI-high) + active T cells (25% PD1+, 1.5:1 ratio). Cytotoxicity = (control - experiment)/control x 100 on final tumor count, paired per seed.", "expected_result": "Fewer tumor cells with T cells than in the no-T control (positive mean cytotoxicity).", "main_expert_question": "How does cytotoxicity scale with the T:tumor ratio and PDL1+ fraction toward the paper's dose curves?"}, "biological_summary": "This mirrors the paper's in-vitro cytotoxicity assay (Hickey, Agmon et al., Cell Systems\n2024, Figure 2). There, PMEL-activated CD8+ T cells were co-cultured 1:1 with B16-F10\nmelanoma cells that were either untreated or IFNg-pretreated; the IFNg-pretreated (PDL1+,\nMHC-I-high) tumors were killed far more effectively (~50% vs ~7% cytotoxicity at 13 h), and\nthe multiscale model reproduced this (~60% cytotoxicity by 12 h, mean of n=5 \u00b1 SEM), where\ncytotoxicity = (control \u2212 experiment)/control \u00d7 100. Here, tumor cells (50% PDL1+) were\nco-cultured with active T cells (25% PD1+) at a 1.5:1 ratio with a matched no-T control\nacross 6 seeds; mean cytotoxicity was positive (~11% \u00b1 8, positive in 5/6 seeds) \u2014 net\nkilling from cumulative cytotoxic-packet delivery to MHC-I-high tumors. The dense, high-ratio\nwell-mixed layout gives enough sustained contact for killing to register, unlike the sparser\nspatial microenvironment.\n", "expected_behavior": [{"name": "positive_cytotoxicity", "en": "Mean net tumor reduction vs. the matched no-T control is positive.", "measure": {"kind": "scalar", "of": "cytotoxicity = (control_final - experiment_final)/control_final x 100, mean over seeds"}, "expect": {"op": "positive", "of": "mean cytotoxicity > 0"}}], "behavior_tests": [{"name": "positive_cytotoxicity", "measure": {"kind": "scalar", "of": "paired cytotoxicity % across 6 seeds"}, "expect": {"op": "positive", "of": "mean cytotoxicity > 0"}, "result": "PASS: cytotoxicity ~11 \u00b1 8% (positive in 5/6 seeds)"}], "report": {"title": "Killing / cytotoxicity assay", "verdict": "passing-with-caveats", "confidence": "medium", "evidence_quality": "replicated", "objective": "Show positive, replicated T-cell cytotoxicity vs. a matched control.", "conclusion": "With a 1.5:1 T:tumor ratio against 50% PDL1+ tumors, active T cells reduce the final tumor\ncount relative to the no-T control by ~11% on average (positive in 5/6 seeds).\n", "caveat": "Single ratio / PDL1+ fraction and reduced scale; a ratio + PDL1+ sweep with more replicates\nis needed to reproduce the paper's quantitative cytotoxicity dose curves.\n"}, "readouts": ["tumor count over time (+T vs control), mean \u00b1 std", "paired cytotoxicity % across seeds"], "canonical_runs": [{"name": "killing-vs-control", "script": "studies/killing-assay-cytotoxicity/sims/run.py"}], "visualizations": [{"name": "tumor_count_vs_control", "path": "viz/tumor_count_vs_control.html"}, {"name": "population_tumor", "path": "viz/population_tumor.html"}, {"name": "deaths", "path": "viz/deaths.html"}, {"name": "spatial_killing", "path": "viz/spatial_killing.html"}, {"name": "spatial_killing_demo", "path": "viz/spatial_killing_demo.html"}], "baseline": [{"name": "baseline", "composite": "viva_tumor_tcell.composites.microenvironment.killing_assay", "params": {"n_tumors": 30, "tumor_t_ratio": 1.5, "pdl1_positive_frac": 0.5, "pd1_positive_frac": 0.25}}], "interface": {"composite": null, "c